1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,021
In the summer of 751,

2
00:00:02,021 --> 00:00:04,683
two of the largest empires on Earth

3
00:00:04,683 --> 00:00:07,097
met for the first and only time

4
00:00:07,097 --> 00:00:07,902
in battle.

5
00:00:08,222 --> 00:00:09,869
On one side, Tang China,

6
00:00:09,869 --> 00:00:11,955
the richest state in the world.

7
00:00:12,275 --> 00:00:15,184
On the other, a brand-new Islamic dynasty,

8
00:00:15,184 --> 00:00:16,115
the Abbasids.

9
00:00:16,435 --> 00:00:18,331
They fought for five days by a

10
00:00:18,331 --> 00:00:19,678
river in Central Asia.

11
00:00:19,898 --> 00:00:21,022
On the fifth day,

12
00:00:21,022 --> 00:00:23,525
a third of the battlefield changed sides.

13
00:00:23,745 --> 00:00:25,522
This is the Battle of Talas,

14
00:00:25,522 --> 00:00:28,111
and almost everything you've heard about it

15
00:00:28,111 --> 00:00:30,294
is more complicated than the legend.

16
00:00:30,514 --> 00:00:32,595
If you like stories that check the

17
00:00:32,595 --> 00:00:34,776
facts, hit like, it really helps the

18
00:00:34,776 --> 00:00:35,271
channel.

19
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:39,093
Look at the map.

20
00:00:39,313 --> 00:00:41,804
Here is Chang'an, the Tang capital,

21
00:00:41,804 --> 00:00:42,812
today's Xi'an.

22
00:00:43,032 --> 00:00:45,665
Here is Baghdad, which the Abbasids would

23
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:47,277
found eleven years later.

24
00:00:47,747 --> 00:00:51,063
Between them, three thousand miles of desert,

25
00:00:51,063 --> 00:00:53,128
mountains, and oasis cities:

26
00:00:53,128 --> 00:00:55,193
Samarkand, Bukhara, Tashkent.

27
00:00:55,413 --> 00:00:58,311
Whoever controlled these cities controlled the Silk

28
00:00:58,311 --> 00:00:58,655
Road.

29
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:01,021
The battle took place here,

30
00:01:01,021 --> 00:01:02,859
in the Talas River valley,

31
00:01:02,859 --> 00:01:04,984
near the modern city of Taraz,

32
00:01:04,984 --> 00:01:08,085
right on today's border between Kazakhstan and

33
00:01:08,085 --> 00:01:08,832
Kyrgyzstan.

34
00:01:09,052 --> 00:01:11,313
The exact field has never been found.

35
00:01:11,783 --> 00:01:14,244
By the 740s the Tang dynasty had

36
00:01:14,244 --> 00:01:17,320
pushed further west than any Chinese state

37
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:18,119
before it.

38
00:01:18,339 --> 00:01:20,609
Its best general in the west was

39
00:01:20,609 --> 00:01:23,502
Gao Xianzhi, a commander of Korean descent.

40
00:01:23,972 --> 00:01:25,990
He had led an army across the

41
00:01:25,990 --> 00:01:28,663
Pamir mountains into what is now northern

42
00:01:28,663 --> 00:01:29,263
Pakistan.

43
00:01:29,483 --> 00:01:31,679
That march is still studied as one

44
00:01:31,679 --> 00:01:34,347
of the great mountain campaigns in history.

45
00:01:34,817 --> 00:01:36,376
Meanwhile in the west,

46
00:01:36,376 --> 00:01:39,553
the Umayyad Caliphate had just been overthrown.

47
00:01:39,773 --> 00:01:42,912
In 750 a revolution that started in

48
00:01:42,912 --> 00:01:46,854
Khorasan, eastern Iran, put the Abbasid family

49
00:01:46,854 --> 00:01:47,730
in power.

50
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,324
Their strongman in the east was Abu

51
00:01:50,324 --> 00:01:52,864
Muslim, the man who had organised that

52
00:01:52,864 --> 00:01:53,582
revolution.

53
00:01:56,252 --> 00:01:58,556
The war started with a local quarrel.

54
00:01:58,776 --> 00:02:02,038
In 750 Gao Xianzhi attacked the kingdom

55
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:04,259
of Shash, today's Tashkent.

56
00:02:04,479 --> 00:02:07,197
Its ruler was taken to Chang'an and

57
00:02:07,197 --> 00:02:07,892
executed.

58
00:02:08,362 --> 00:02:10,900
His son escaped and asked the Abbasids

59
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:11,562
for help.

60
00:02:11,782 --> 00:02:14,098
Abu Muslim saw a chance and sent

61
00:02:14,098 --> 00:02:16,241
an army under Ziyad ibn Salih.

62
00:02:16,461 --> 00:02:19,042
Gao decided to strike first and marched

63
00:02:19,042 --> 00:02:19,426
west.

64
00:02:22,096 --> 00:02:24,123
Here's where you have to be careful.

65
00:02:24,343 --> 00:02:28,022
Arab writers said the Chinese brought 100,000

66
00:02:28,022 --> 00:02:28,439
men.

67
00:02:28,659 --> 00:02:31,233
Chinese records say about 30,000:

68
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:34,269
perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 Tang soldiers,

69
00:02:34,269 --> 00:02:36,382
and the rest Karluk Turks,

70
00:02:36,382 --> 00:02:39,352
nomads from the steppe who fought for

71
00:02:39,352 --> 00:02:39,880
China.

72
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:43,830
Most modern historians think something like 30,000

73
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:46,110
on each side is more realistic.

74
00:02:46,330 --> 00:02:49,383
Medieval numbers are almost always inflated,

75
00:02:49,383 --> 00:02:52,197
so treat any big figure with suspicion.

76
00:02:52,417 --> 00:02:54,251
That is true for both sides.

77
00:02:56,921 --> 00:02:58,956
The armies met by the Talas River

78
00:02:58,956 --> 00:03:01,189
and fought for several days without a

79
00:03:01,189 --> 00:03:01,636
winner.

80
00:03:01,856 --> 00:03:04,410
Tang crossbowmen were feared across Asia,

81
00:03:04,410 --> 00:03:06,485
and Gao's men held their ground.

82
00:03:06,955 --> 00:03:09,969
Tang infantry carried powerful crossbows,

83
00:03:09,969 --> 00:03:13,623
and Chinese military manuals describe firing them

84
00:03:13,623 --> 00:03:16,251
in rotating ranks: one row shoots,

85
00:03:16,251 --> 00:03:18,046
the next steps forward,

86
00:03:18,046 --> 00:03:19,393
the last reloads.

87
00:03:19,613 --> 00:03:21,882
For four days that wall of bolts

88
00:03:21,882 --> 00:03:22,279
held.

89
00:03:22,749 --> 00:03:24,079
Then, on the fifth day,

90
00:03:24,079 --> 00:03:25,684
some accounts say the fourth,

91
00:03:25,684 --> 00:03:26,739
the Karluks turned.

92
00:03:26,959 --> 00:03:29,264
They attacked the Tang army from behind

93
00:03:29,264 --> 00:03:31,716
while the Abbasids pressed from the front.

94
00:03:31,936 --> 00:03:34,370
The Karluks were around two-thirds of Gao's

95
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:34,752
force.

96
00:03:35,222 --> 00:03:37,569
Chinese chronicles call it treachery.

97
00:03:37,789 --> 00:03:40,458
Some Arab historians say the Karluks had

98
00:03:40,458 --> 00:03:42,461
been Abbasid allies all along.

99
00:03:42,681 --> 00:03:44,609
We honestly don't know if it was

100
00:03:44,609 --> 00:03:45,091
planned.

101
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:46,456
What do you think:

102
00:03:46,456 --> 00:03:48,597
betrayal, or a deal made before the

103
00:03:48,597 --> 00:03:49,045
battle?

104
00:03:49,265 --> 00:03:50,715
Write it in the comments.

105
00:03:53,385 --> 00:03:55,146
Caught between two enemies,

106
00:03:55,146 --> 00:03:56,521
the Tang army broke.

107
00:03:56,741 --> 00:03:59,961
Chinese sources say 20,000 to 30,000 men

108
00:03:59,961 --> 00:04:02,979
were killed and fewer than 2,000 made

109
00:04:02,979 --> 00:04:03,717
it home.

110
00:04:04,187 --> 00:04:07,820
Arab sources claim 50,000 dead and 20,000

111
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:08,710
prisoners.

112
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:11,490
Those Arab numbers are bigger than the

113
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:13,827
whole army Chinese records describe,

114
00:04:13,827 --> 00:04:16,275
which tells you how much the sources

115
00:04:16,275 --> 00:04:16,887
disagree.

116
00:04:17,357 --> 00:04:19,149
Gao Xianzhi escaped.

117
00:04:19,369 --> 00:04:22,117
In the narrow mountain passes his officer

118
00:04:22,117 --> 00:04:24,192
Li Siye cut a way through the

119
00:04:24,192 --> 00:04:27,107
crowd of fleeing soldiers with the remaining

120
00:04:27,107 --> 00:04:27,668
cavalry.

121
00:04:27,888 --> 00:04:30,134
Gao lived, but he would never lead

122
00:04:30,134 --> 00:04:32,005
an army this far west again.

123
00:04:34,675 --> 00:04:36,813
One of the captured men was a

124
00:04:36,813 --> 00:04:38,835
young officer called Du Huan.

125
00:04:39,055 --> 00:04:41,772
He spent around ten years in the

126
00:04:41,772 --> 00:04:44,896
Abbasid world, was released in 762 and

127
00:04:44,896 --> 00:04:47,205
made his way back to China.

128
00:04:47,675 --> 00:04:49,684
He wrote about what he saw:

129
00:04:49,684 --> 00:04:53,053
Chinese silk weavers and painters already working

130
00:04:53,053 --> 00:04:54,944
in Abbasid cities, mosques,

131
00:04:54,944 --> 00:04:57,959
and customs the Chinese had never described

132
00:04:57,959 --> 00:04:58,491
before.

133
00:04:58,711 --> 00:05:01,246
Only fragments of his book survive,

134
00:05:01,246 --> 00:05:04,023
quoted in a later Chinese encyclopedia.

135
00:05:04,243 --> 00:05:06,424
It is one of the earliest Chinese

136
00:05:06,424 --> 00:05:08,979
eyewitness accounts of the Islamic world.

137
00:05:11,649 --> 00:05:12,630
Myth check.

138
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:15,457
You may have read that Chinese prisoners

139
00:05:15,457 --> 00:05:18,172
from Talas taught papermaking in Samarkand,

140
00:05:18,172 --> 00:05:20,398
and that is how paper reached the

141
00:05:20,398 --> 00:05:22,407
Islamic world and later Europe.

142
00:05:22,627 --> 00:05:24,142
It's a great story.

143
00:05:24,612 --> 00:05:27,380
The problem: it first appears in a

144
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:31,071
writer named al-Tha'alibi, about 250 years after

145
00:05:31,071 --> 00:05:31,993
the battle.

146
00:05:32,213 --> 00:05:35,136
No Chinese source and no contemporary Arab

147
00:05:35,136 --> 00:05:36,480
source mentions it.

148
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:40,135
And paper was already known in Central

149
00:05:40,135 --> 00:05:42,076
Asia long before 751.

150
00:05:42,296 --> 00:05:43,577
Talas may have helped,

151
00:05:43,577 --> 00:05:45,475
but it probably wasn't the start.

152
00:05:48,145 --> 00:05:51,188
Many videos say Talas decided that Central

153
00:05:51,188 --> 00:05:54,353
Asia would become Muslim instead of Chinese.

154
00:05:54,573 --> 00:05:56,578
The truth is more interesting.

155
00:05:56,798 --> 00:05:58,228
Just one year later,

156
00:05:58,228 --> 00:06:00,745
Abbasid envoys were back in Chang'an.

157
00:06:01,215 --> 00:06:04,455
What really ended Chinese power in the

158
00:06:04,455 --> 00:06:06,779
west came four years later,

159
00:06:06,779 --> 00:06:09,527
in 755: the An Lushan rebellion,

160
00:06:09,527 --> 00:06:12,837
a civil war inside China so destructive

161
00:06:12,837 --> 00:06:16,148
that the Tang pulled their armies home.

162
00:06:16,368 --> 00:06:18,659
As historian James Millward put it,

163
00:06:18,659 --> 00:06:20,733
it was not the defeat at Talas

164
00:06:20,733 --> 00:06:23,024
that forced the Chinese to retreat.

165
00:06:23,494 --> 00:06:25,329
Look at the map then and now.

166
00:06:25,549 --> 00:06:28,650
In 751 Tang garrisons stood at the

167
00:06:28,650 --> 00:06:30,349
edge of Kyrgyzstan.

168
00:06:30,569 --> 00:06:33,690
It would take about a thousand years,

169
00:06:33,690 --> 00:06:37,297
until the Qing dynasty conquered Xinjiang in

170
00:06:37,297 --> 00:06:40,834
1759, before a Chinese state ruled Xinjiang

171
00:06:40,834 --> 00:06:42,985
again, just east of here.

172
00:06:45,655 --> 00:06:47,297
And the men of Talas?

173
00:06:47,517 --> 00:06:49,633
Most of them did not live to

174
00:06:49,633 --> 00:06:50,632
enjoy victory.

175
00:06:50,852 --> 00:06:53,743
Abu Muslim, the man who sent the

176
00:06:53,743 --> 00:06:57,213
Abbasid army, was murdered on the orders

177
00:06:57,213 --> 00:06:59,599
of Caliph al-Mansur in 755.

178
00:06:59,819 --> 00:07:02,016
He had simply become too powerful.

179
00:07:02,486 --> 00:07:04,465
Gao Xianzhi survived Talas,

180
00:07:04,465 --> 00:07:06,134
but not the rebellion.

181
00:07:06,354 --> 00:07:09,274
Early in 756, after retreating to Tong

182
00:07:09,274 --> 00:07:11,369
Pass to defend the capital,

183
00:07:11,369 --> 00:07:14,353
he was executed on the emperor's orders

184
00:07:14,353 --> 00:07:16,893
after a court eunuch accused him.

185
00:07:17,113 --> 00:07:20,142
Many historians think the charges were unfair.

186
00:07:20,362 --> 00:07:22,137
His officer Li Siye,

187
00:07:22,137 --> 00:07:24,550
the man who cut a path out

188
00:07:24,550 --> 00:07:27,744
at Talas, died fighting the rebels in

189
00:07:27,744 --> 00:07:28,170
759.

190
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:30,675
And the Karluks, the side-switchers,

191
00:07:30,675 --> 00:07:32,032
may have won the most.

192
00:07:32,252 --> 00:07:35,053
By 766 they had taken Suyab and

193
00:07:35,053 --> 00:07:38,070
the lands around the Chu and Talas

194
00:07:38,070 --> 00:07:38,716
rivers.

195
00:07:38,936 --> 00:07:41,796
One theory links them to the Kara-Khanids,

196
00:07:41,796 --> 00:07:44,313
one of the first Turkic dynasties to

197
00:07:44,313 --> 00:07:46,659
adopt Islam, in the tenth century.

198
00:07:46,879 --> 00:07:48,543
That link is still debated.

199
00:07:49,013 --> 00:07:51,484
Today, the Talas valley is part of

200
00:07:51,484 --> 00:07:54,543
Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan, and the cities of

201
00:07:54,543 --> 00:07:57,132
Taraz and Talas stand near the place

202
00:07:57,132 --> 00:07:59,721
where two empires met once and never

203
00:07:59,721 --> 00:08:00,191
again.

204
00:08:00,661 --> 00:08:02,568
So who really won at Talas:

205
00:08:02,568 --> 00:08:05,540
the Abbasids, the Karluks who switched sides,

206
00:08:05,540 --> 00:08:08,063
or the rebellion that came four years

207
00:08:08,063 --> 00:08:08,512
later?

208
00:08:08,732 --> 00:08:10,183
Tell me in the comments.

209
00:08:10,403 --> 00:08:12,527
And if you want the story of

210
00:08:12,527 --> 00:08:15,419
what happened when the Mongols arrived in

211
00:08:15,419 --> 00:08:17,780
this same region 470 years later,

212
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:20,259
subscribe and turn on notifications.

213
00:08:20,479 --> 00:08:22,356
That episode is coming soon.

